\f0\fs48 \cf2 Around 1668, Mariotte (who helped found the French Academy of Sciences) reported on the blind spot.  He thought that the choroid of the eye was the light-sensitive part, because the choroid layer halted at the margin of the optic nerve head.  An understandable error, particularly because the detail of the retina wasn't understood for another two centuries.\
\
Why is there a blindspot?\
\
Working together, your eyes allow you to see across a wide space of the world (around 160 degrees wide).   \
\
Each eye, however, has a small space where it cannot see anything:  this occurs because there needs to be a space (the Optic Nerve Head) to allow neurons from INSIDE the eye to travel OUT OF THE EYE to the brain.  Because there are no photoreceptors at the Optic Nerve Head, there is a blind spot for each eye.  On average, this blind area is around 5 degrees wide, and lies about 15 degrees to the right of the straight-ahead position (for the right eye) and 15 degrees to the left of the straight-ahead position for the left eye.  \
\
Under normal conditions, you are unaware of this non-seeing area, because the vision of each eye covers the non-seeing area of the other eye.  \
\
When you are looking with one eye alone, however, the brain fills in the missing area with detail from the rest of the image in the eye.  \
\
Taking a look at your own blindspot will give you a chance to test this out.     }